::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which number is greatest? {
~A. -9#This is the farthest left value, so it is the least, not the greatest.
=B. 4#Correct. Positive 4 is to the right of the negative values and zero.
~C. -2#This is greater than -9 but still less than any positive number.
~D. 0#Zero is greater than negative numbers but less than 4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which comparison is true? {
~A. -2 &lt; -7#This reverses the order. -2 is farther right, so it is greater than -7.
~B. -7 &gt; 0#A negative number is less than zero.
=C. -7 &lt; -2#Correct. -7 is farther left on the number line than -2.
~D. 0 &lt; -2#Zero is greater than any negative number.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the opposite of -11? {
~A. -11#This is the original number, not its opposite.
~B. 0#Zero is not the opposite of -11.
~C. -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>11</sub>#This is a reciprocal-like error, not the opposite.
=D. 11#Correct. Opposites are the same distance from zero on opposite sides of the number line.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate |-13|. {
=A. 13#Correct. -13 is 13 units from zero.
~B. -13#Absolute value is distance from zero, so it cannot be negative.
~C. 0#The number is not zero units from zero.
~D. 26#This doubles the distance instead of finding the distance from zero.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Order from least to greatest: -3, 5, -8, 0. {
~A. -3, -8, 0, 5#This puts -3 before -8, but -8 is farther left and is smaller.
~B. 5, 0, -3, -8#This orders from greatest to least.
=C. -8, -3, 0, 5#Correct. From left to right on the number line, the order is -8, -3, 0, 5.
~D. -8, 0, -3, 5#This places 0 before -3, but 0 is greater than -3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -9 + 4. {
~A. -13#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. The signs are different, so subtract the absolute values.
=B. -5#Correct. The signs are different, so 9 - 4 = 5, and the negative number has the larger absolute value.
~C. 5#This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~D. 13#This adds absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -6 + (-8). {
=A. -14#Correct. Same signs mean add the absolute values and keep the negative sign.
~B. -2#This subtracts the absolute values, but the signs are the same.
~C. 2#This subtracts and uses a positive sign, which does not match adding two negatives.
~D. 14#This adds the absolute values but uses the wrong sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 12 + (-15). {
~A. -27#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. Different signs require subtraction.
~B. 3#This subtracts correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~C. 27#This adds the absolute values and uses a positive sign.
=D. -3#Correct. The signs are different, so 15 - 12 = 3, and the negative value has the larger absolute value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 7 - 11. {
~A. -18#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. The expression is subtraction.
~B. 4#This reverses the subtraction.
=C. -4#Correct. 7 - 11 means move 11 units left from 7, ending at -4.
~D. 18#This adds the numbers instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 8 - (-5). {
~A. 3#This treats subtracting a negative as ordinary subtraction.
=B. 13#Correct. Subtracting a negative means adding the opposite: 8 + 5 = 13.
~C. -13#This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~D. -3#This subtracts and then uses a negative sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -4 - 9. {
~A. -5#This subtracts the absolute values instead of moving left.
~B. 5#This ignores the negative direction.
~C. 13#This uses the right distance but the wrong sign.
=D. -13#Correct. -4 - 9 means move 9 more units left from -4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -3 - (-10). {
=A. 7#Correct. -3 - (-10) = -3 + 10 = 7.
~B. -13#This treats both values as negatives being added.
~C. -7#This uses the correct distance but wrong direction.
~D. 13#This adds absolute values instead of adding the opposite.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (-5)(6). {
~A. -11#This adds the numbers instead of multiplying them.
~B. 30#This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign.
=C. -30#Correct. Different signs make a negative product, and 5 &times; 6 = 30.
~D. 11#This adds absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (-7)(-2). {
~A. -14#This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign. Same signs make a positive product.
=B. 14#Correct. A negative times a negative is positive, and 7 &times; 2 = 14.
~C. -9#This adds the values instead of multiplying.
~D. 9#This adds absolute values instead of multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -24 &divide; 6. {
=A. -4#Correct. Different signs make a negative quotient, and 24 &divide; 6 = 4.
~B. 4#This divides correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~C. -18#This subtracts instead of dividing.
~D. 30#This adds absolute values instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -36 &divide; (-9). {
~A. -4#This divides correctly but uses the wrong sign. Same signs make a positive quotient.
=B. 4#Correct. A negative divided by a negative is positive, and 36 &divide; 9 = 4.
~C. -27#This subtracts instead of dividing.
~D. 45#This adds absolute values instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has a positive result? {
~A. (-3)(5)#Different signs make a negative product.
~B. -3 - 5#This moves farther left and gives a negative result.
~C. -3 + (-5)#Adding two negatives gives a negative result.
=D. (-3)(-5)#Correct. Same signs make a positive product.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has a negative result? {
=A. (-8)(3)#Correct. Different signs make a negative product.
~B. (-8)(-3)#Same signs make a positive product.
~C. 8 + 3#Adding two positives gives a positive result.
~D. 8 - (-3)#Subtracting a negative means adding, so the result is positive.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. {
~A. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>#This adds numerators and denominators, which is not how fractions are added.
~B. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This multiplies denominators instead of finding a common denominator.
=C. <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#Correct. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, and <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>.
~D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#This subtracts instead of adding.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. {
~A. -1#This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign.
=B. -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>#Correct. -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, which equals -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>.
~C. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>#This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~D. -<sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This equals -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>, but it is not written in simplest form.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> - <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>. {
=A. -1#Correct. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> - <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> = -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> = -1.
~B. 1#This subtracts in the wrong order.
~C. <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>0</sub>#This subtracts denominators, which is not valid.
~D. -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>#This subtracts denominators incorrectly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> &times; 10. {
~A. -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub>#This multiplies the denominator by 10 instead of multiplying the value.
~B. 4#This finds the size but loses the negative sign.
~C. -12#This subtracts instead of multiplying.
=D. -4#Correct. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> of 10 is 4, and the negative sign makes the result -4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A temperature changes from -3°F to 5°F. What is the change in temperature? {
~A. -8°F#This subtracts in the wrong direction.
~B. 2°F#This adds -3 and 5 instead of finding the change.
=C. 8°F#Correct. Moving from -3 to 5 is an increase of 8 degrees.
~D. -2°F#This adds the values and keeps a negative sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says -10 is greater than -4 because 10 is greater than 4. What is the best correction? {
=A. -10 is less because it is farther left on the number line.#Correct. Number line position determines which signed number is greater or less.
~B. -10 is greater because it has the larger absolute value.#This repeats the misconception. For negative numbers, larger absolute value means farther left.
~C. -10 and -4 are equal because both are negative.#Negative numbers can have different values.
~D. -4 is less because 4 is smaller than 10.#This ignores the negative signs and number line positions.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student evaluates 6 - (-2) as 4. What is the best correction? {
~A. The answer is 4 because subtracting always makes a number smaller.#This is the misconception. Subtracting a negative increases the value.
~B. 6 - (-2) = -8 because two negative signs make the answer negative.#Two signs change the operation here; they do not make the answer negative.
~C. 6 - (-2) = 12 because the signs mean multiply.#The expression is subtraction, not multiplication.
=D. 6 - (-2) = 6 + 2 = 8 because subtracting a negative means adding the opposite.#Correct. The opposite of -2 is 2, so the expression becomes 6 + 2.
}
